WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.500
 

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:09.240
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:10.300
 

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:13.000
DAVE WILLIAMS: Have you ever
heard of the 80-20 rule?

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.970
While its roots are in economics
and process improvement,

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:18.160
it's found its way into popular culture.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:22.170
The basic idea is that with
everything we are trying to improve,

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:26.330
80% of the problem or opportunity
lies with 20% of the reasons.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:28.230
So what is a Pareto chart?

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.310
Well, let's take a look.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:34.289
A Pareto chart is both
a bar and a line chart.

00:00:34.289 --> 00:00:35.580
So we're going to draw this up.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.740
And it has two axes.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:42.350
So there's the y-axis
on the left hand side.

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:43.530
We got our x-axis.

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:46.135
And then we've got another
y-axis on the right hand side.

00:00:46.135 --> 00:00:49.330
 

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:54.197
On the right hand side is the
percentage in which something occurs.

00:00:54.197 --> 00:00:56.280
So we're just going to try
to keep it simple here.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:04.670
We'll put 100%, 50%, and
then just down here at 0.

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:08.770
On the left hand side, this is the
frequency in which an issue occurs.

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:10.900
So again, we'll try to
keep it simple here.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:17.210
And we'll go with 10,
5, and that would be 0.

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:22.281
And then along the bottom, we list
the categories or defects of issues

00:01:22.281 --> 00:01:23.030
that we're seeing.

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:31.304
So A, B, C, D, E. And
I'll just put the defects.

00:01:31.304 --> 00:01:34.150
 

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:38.894
Now as we're tracking our data from
a collection form, we'll look at it

00:01:38.894 --> 00:01:41.560
and we'll say, well gosh, how
many did we have of each of these?

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:47.071
And we try to list these in the order
in which is most frequent to least

00:01:47.071 --> 00:01:47.570
frequent.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:49.540
So it's showing you from big to small.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:52.800
So if we think of Defect
A, let's imagine that we

00:01:52.800 --> 00:02:00.210
had 8 of Defect A. Defect B, we had 5.

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:03.470
Defect C, we only had about 3.

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:05.970
Defect D, there was maybe 2.

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:09.259
And Defect E had 1.

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:18.032
So again, we'll-- And then there's
another piece that's a line chart.

00:02:18.032 --> 00:02:19.740
And that's where the
percentages come in.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:23.690
And what we're trying to do is identify
what percentage of the total problem

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:29.310
is each of the defects, so we can try
to see which is the most common to least

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:29.810
common.

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:31.970
We're looking for that 80-20 rule.

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:37.200
So imagine we've got this line chart.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:42.710
And we'll just say that,
if this is 80 here,

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:46.080
if we work on these
particular defect, we

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.982
can expect that we can actually make
the biggest improvement in our process.

00:02:49.982 --> 00:02:51.690
And so it's a great
place for us to start

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:55.400
doing our PDSA testing and some
of our other tools and methods

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:58.400
to identify opportunities
for improvement.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:01.950
So the idea here is to look at the
issues with the biggest effect--

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:06.740
the vital few 20%-- and focus
your improvements there first.

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:10.710
If successful, your initial improvements
will make the biggest difference.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:14.940
Pareto charts are very easy to
create in any spreadsheet program

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.460
and are helpful for converting check
sheet and cause and effect data

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:20.100
into knowledge.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:24.060
It helps focus improvement efforts
on the vital few opportunities

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:27.790
for improvement and can
help you make a big impact.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:40.960
 

